This was a long time in the making, but a NetHack with proper
internationalization support is now available at

https://sourceforge.net/projects/nethack-i18n/ .

This is as far as I know the first ever multilingual roguelike; it supports
English and Spanish, and should be extensible to other locales.  (I've quite
extensively refactored the English code since I last played in English, and may
have introduced some bugs.  I'll want to run another test game, and I'll be back
when (not if:-/) I find any more bugs.  I did just ascend a wizard in Spanish.)

NetHack-i18n presently supports only Linux and only a curses interface, enhanced
to support full IBMgraphics.  I plan to expand this to Windows and a graphical
interface for both.  The LANG environment variable chooses the language in the
standard fashion.  I had to install the Spanish locale to get this to work on
Ubuntu.

The i18n is done using the old familiar gettext tools, paired with a Ruby 1.9
scripted printf.  The script knows what language you're using and handles such
things as plurals, verb tenses, gender inflections, and words that change form
or contract according to what precedes or follows.

As for variants -- I rather like what NetHack 4 has done to reorganize and clean
up the code.  I'll probably want to make NetHack-i18n look a lot like NetHack 4,
up to and including even a NetHack 4 i18n job.



